<!-- HOW IT WORKS -->

<section>

  <h2 class="section-title">
    How the Stirling Engine Works
  </h2>

  <p class="section-intro">
    The engine converts heat into mechanical motion by repeatedly heating
    and cooling the trapped air inside the engine.
  </p>


  <div class="process-grid">


    <div class="process-step">

      <div class="step-number">
        STEP 01
      </div>

      <h3>
        Heat
      </h3>

      <p>
        A temperature difference is created between the bottom hot plate
        and the cooler top plate.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="process-step">

      <div class="step-number">
        STEP 02
      </div>

      <h3>
        Expand
      </h3>

      <p>
        The displacer moves air toward the hot side, causing the trapped
        air to heat and expand.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="process-step">

      <div class="step-number">
        STEP 03
      </div>

      <h3>
        Produce Motion
      </h3>

      <p>
        The expanding air pushes the piston, which drives the crankshaft
        and rotates the flywheel.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="process-step">

      <div class="step-number">
        STEP 04
      </div>

      <h3>
        Cool & Repeat
      </h3>

      <p>
        The displacer moves the air toward the cool side, where it contracts,
        allowing the cycle to repeat continuously.
      </p>

    </div>


  </div>

</section>


<!-- THERMODYNAMIC PRINCIPLES -->

<section>

  <h2 class="section-title">
    Engineering Principles
  </h2>


  <div class="two-column">


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        First Law of Thermodynamics
      </h3>

      <p>
        The engine converts heat input into mechanical work. Because
        the engine operates cyclically, the working gas returns to its
        initial state after each complete cycle.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        Temperature Difference
      </h3>

      <p>
        A thermal gradient is required for operation. Heat naturally
        transfers from the hotter region toward the colder region,
        allowing the engine to produce useful mechanical motion.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        Ideal Gas Behavior
      </h3>

      <p>
        The air inside the engine expands when heated and contracts
        when cooled. These pressure and volume changes drive the piston.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        Flywheel Momentum
      </h3>

      <p>
        The flywheel stores rotational energy and helps maintain
        smooth continuous motion as the engine moves through each
        stage of its thermodynamic cycle.
      </p>

    </div>


  </div>

</section>


<!-- MECHANICAL DISSECTION -->

<section>

  <h2 class="section-title">
    Mechanical Dissection
  </h2>

  <p class="section-intro">
    The engine was carefully disassembled to identify the major components,
    understand their functions, and examine how the thermal and mechanical
    subsystems interact.
  </p>


  <div class="components-grid">


    <div class="component-card">

      <div class="component-detail">
        PISTON ASSEMBLY
      </div>

      <h3>
        Piston & Connecting Rod
      </h3>

      <p>
        The glass piston responds to pressure changes in the working air.
        Its linear motion is transferred through the connecting rod
        to the crankshaft.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="component-card">

      <div class="component-detail">
        ROTATING ASSEMBLY
      </div>

      <h3>
        Crankshaft & Flywheel
      </h3>

      <p>
        The crankshaft converts the piston's linear movement into
        rotational motion while the flywheel stores rotational energy
        and helps maintain smooth operation.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="component-card">

      <div class="component-detail">
        THERMAL SYSTEM
      </div>

      <h3>
        Top & Bottom Plates
      </h3>

      <p>
        The bottom plate acts as the hot interface while the upper
        assembly serves as the cooler side of the engine, creating
        the thermal gradient required for operation.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="component-card">

      <div class="component-detail">
        AIR TRANSFER
      </div>

      <h3>
        Displacer & Cylinder
      </h3>

      <p>
        The displacer moves the working air between the hot and cold
        regions. The transparent cylinder encloses the working volume
        while allowing the internal mechanism to remain visible.
      </p>

    </div>


  </div>

</section>


<!-- EXPERIMENT -->

<section>

  <h2 class="section-title">
    Experiment Design
  </h2>

  <p class="section-intro">
    I designed an experiment to determine the relationship between
    flywheel RPM and the temperature difference across the engine.
    Room temperature was held at approximately 22°C while the bottom
    plate temperature was varied using hot water.
  </p>


  <div class="workflow">


    <div class="workflow-step">

      <div class="step-number">
        STEP 01
      </div>

      <h3>
        Control Temperature
      </h3>

      <p>
        Room temperature was maintained at approximately 22°C to keep
        the upper plate near a consistent cold-reservoir temperature.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="workflow-step">

      <div class="step-number">
        STEP 02
      </div>

      <h3>
        Heat Engine
      </h3>

      <p>
        The engine was placed over hot water and allowed approximately
        three minutes for the bottom plate to heat.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="workflow-step">

      <div class="step-number">
        STEP 03
      </div>

      <h3>
        Measure
      </h3>

      <p>
        Water temperature was measured and flywheel rotations were
        counted over a 10-second interval.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="workflow-step">

      <div class="step-number">
        STEP 04
      </div>

      <h3>
        Repeat & Analyze
      </h3>

      <p>
        Three trials were performed at each condition and the process
        was repeated across several temperature differences.
      </p>

    </div>


  </div>


  <div class="ice-box">

    <h3>
      Ice Trial
    </h3>

    <p>
      An additional trial placed the engine over an ice block instead
      of hot water. This reversed the thermal gradient so the bottom
      plate became colder than the top plate. The flywheel responded
      by rotating in the opposite direction, producing a measured
      speed of -70 RPM at a ΔT of -21.5°C.
    </p>

  </div>

</section>


<!-- DATA ANALYSIS -->

<section>

  <h2 class="section-title">
    RPM vs. Temperature Difference
  </h2>

  <p class="section-intro">
    The experimental results showed a strong approximately linear
    relationship between temperature difference and flywheel speed.
    Greater temperature differences produced higher rotational speeds.
  </p>


  <div class="chart-card">

    <svg
      id="rpmChart"
      viewBox="0 0 760 420"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Graph of flywheel RPM versus temperature difference"
    ></svg>

    <div class="chart-caption">
      Experimental RPM measurements compared with the linear regression model.
    </div>

  </div>


  <div class="analysis-box">

    <h3>
      Linear Regression
    </h3>

    <p>
      The measured data produced the relationship:
    </p>

    <div class="equation">
      RPM = 3.26(ΔT) − 10.8
    </div>

    <p>
      Setting RPM equal to zero gives the estimated minimum
      temperature difference required for startup:
    </p>

    <div class="equation">
      ΔT ≈ 3.3°C
    </div>

    <p>
      This means the engine required only a small thermal gradient
      before the flywheel began rotating.
    </p>

  </div>

</section>


