<!-- LIMITATIONS -->

<section>

  <h2 class="section-title">
    Experimental Limitations & Improvements
  </h2>


  <div class="two-column">


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        Heat Loss
      </h3>

      <p>
        The hot-water container was not insulated, allowing heat
        to escape during testing and potentially changing the actual
        temperature experienced by the bottom plate.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        Improved Temperature Measurement
      </h3>

      <p>
        A future version of the experiment could use an insulated
        container and a digital temperature probe mounted directly
        to the engine's bottom plate for more accurate and consistent
        measurements.
      </p>

    </div>


  </div>

</section>


<!-- ENGINEERING TAKEAWAYS -->

<section>

  <h2 class="section-title">
    Engineering Takeaways
  </h2>


  <div class="two-column">


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        Experimental Design
      </h3>

      <p>
        Developing a testing procedure from scratch required deciding
        which variables to control, how to collect repeatable measurements,
        and how to evaluate the resulting data.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        Thermodynamics in Practice
      </h3>

      <p>
        The project demonstrated how a temperature difference can
        produce pressure changes and ultimately mechanical work in
        a functioning heat engine.
      </p>

    </div>


    <div class="card">

      <h3>
        Data Analysis
      </h3>

      <p>
        Experimental measurements revealed a strong linear relationship
